3. BUSINESS LOGIC TO MODEL EXACTLY
---------------------------------------------------------------------
3.1 Prospect sourcing (three routes; always record the source)
- POOL: management uploads prospect lists (CSV import or a Google Sheet, with column mapping, validation and duplicate detection). Reps browse the pool and CLAIM prospects in their region.
- ASSIGNED: a manager or team lead assigns prospects to a rep.
- MANUAL SEARCH: a rep finds dealerships with Google Maps / Places search and adds them. Check for duplicates against existing prospects and clients first.
- TERRITORY RULE: each rep has a home ZIP code. By default, prospects should be within a 60-mile radius of it. Show the distance on every prospect. Flag anything outside the radius, and require a reason or manager approval to claim it. Show the pool on a map with the radius circle.
- CLAIM RULES: show who claimed what and when, auto-release stale claims after a configurable number of days with no activity (with warnings first), and keep a full claim history.

3.2 Visiting
- In-person visits are strongly encouraged. GOOD visit window: 10:00 AM–4:00 PM in the dealership's local time. Visits before 10 AM or after 4 PM are "bad time" visits: allowed, but clearly flagged and tracked in stats.
- Each visit logs check-in (time, optional location), outcome, contact met, notes (voice-to-text supported), products discussed and interest level, next action and follow-up date.

3.3 Pipeline lifecycle (Kanban board + list view, drag and drop, stage-entry rules)
Pool → Claimed/Assigned → Visit Planned → Visited → Follow-up → HOT → Handed off to Manager → Negotiation → Won (verbal yes) → Sign-up / Contract → Signed → Onboarding → CLIENT
Plus: Lost (reason required), Not Now / Nurture (re-contact date), Disqualified.

3.4 Handoff, closing, signing, ownership (critical)
- HOT prospects are usually handed off to a manager to close. The handoff captures the summary, decision maker, objections, products of interest, pricing discussed and an AI-generated briefing.
- Reps can also close deals themselves.
- Once the dealership agrees, sign-up usually goes back to the ORIGIN REP; sometimes the manager completes the signing. Record who signed.
- Whoever looks after the account runs onboarding (a checklist with tasks, owners, due dates and status).
- Once signed, the prospect becomes a CLIENT in the ORIGIN REP's client pool. Origin-rep attribution NEVER changes, even if a manager closed or signed the deal.
- Every record keeps a complete, timestamped audit trail: who scouted, claimed, visited, handed off, closed, signed and onboarded.

3.5 Finance controller
- Reviews the trail for scouting and completed handoffs, and confirms or rejects it (with comments).
- Sets and approves the incentive for each handoff. Amounts VARY per handoff, so it's an editable amount, optionally suggested from configurable rules (rules may vary by product signed or dealership size).
- Incentive states: Pending verification → Verified → Approved → Paid (date + reference) / Rejected / Disputed.
- Reps see their own incentive statement (pending, approved and paid). Finance gets a payout ledger, reports and CSV export by period.

3.6 Client servicing (in the origin rep's client pool)
- Key-fob / keychain restocking (only for clients that use them).
- Upselling new products.
- Following up with the dealership on their sales and promotions.
- A client health score (last contact, order and restock frequency, upsell coverage, issues), with at-risk alerts.

